    "id": "news-1790189072090-3",
    "title": "Somalia Approves $56 Million Youth, Peace and Security Plan",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo ansixisay qorshe $56 milyan ah oo loogu talagalay dhalinyarada",
    "slug": "somalia-approves-56-million-youth-peace-and-security-plan",
    "summary": "The Federal Government of Somalia has finalized a five-year national plan worth $56 million to boost youth participation in peace, security and development.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, 23 September 2026 — The Federal Government of Somalia has completed the approval of its National Youth, Peace and Security Plan for 2026–2030, with a total budget of $56 million. The five-year strategy aims to strengthen the role of Somali youth in peacebuilding, security and national development.\n\nThe plan prioritizes several key areas: youth involvement in politics and peace processes, protection and safety of young people, job creation, conflict prevention, and resilience building. It is designed to address the challenges facing Somalia's large youth population, who have long been sidelined from formal decision-making despite comprising the majority of the country's citizens.\n\nThis initiative follows years of advocacy by Somali youth organizations and international partners, who have pushed for greater inclusion of young people in efforts to stabilize the country. The plan aligns with United Nations Security Council resolutions on youth, peace and security, which call on governments to integrate youth into peace and security frameworks.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the plan represents a significant opportunity to engage with homeland development. Many diaspora youth maintain strong ties to Somalia and have been active in remittances, entrepreneurship and peace advocacy. The new funding could open avenues for diaspora-led projects in job creation and conflict prevention, particularly in sectors such as education, technology and small business.\n\nThe approval also signals a commitment by the federal government to address the root causes of youth radicalization and migration. By investing in youth empowerment, authorities hope to reduce the appeal of extremist groups and provide alternatives to irregular migration, which has affected many Somali families both at home and abroad.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/23/soomaaliya-oo-ansixisay-qorshe-56-milyan-ah-oo-loogu-talagalay-dhalinyarada/",
