    "id": "news-1790189078008-4",
    "title": "Yemen Conflict Displaces Thousands, Hundreds Arrive on Somali Shores",
    "original_title": "Dagaallada Yemen oo boqollaal qof kusoo riixay xeebaha Soomaaliya",
    "slug": "yemen-conflict-displaces-thousands-hundreds-arrive-on-somali-shores",
    "summary": "Ongoing fighting in Yemen has forced over 130,000 people to flee since early September, with hundreds reaching Puntland and Somaliland, according to UNHCR.",
    "content": "Fighting in Yemen has led to the displacement of more than 130,000 people since the beginning of September, the United Nations refugee agency (UNHCR) has reported. Hundreds of those fleeing the conflict have arrived on the coasts of Somalia, particularly in the Puntland and Somaliland regions.\n\nThe displaced include both Yemeni nationals and Somalis who had previously sought refuge in Yemen. According to UNHCR, over 3,000 people have reached Djibouti, while hundreds more have made their way to Somalia. The agency has described the situation as part of a broader regional displacement crisis triggered by the escalating violence in Yemen.\n\nThis is not the first time that conflict in Yemen has driven people to seek safety in the Horn of Africa. Yemen has long hosted Somali refugees, but the current wave of violence has reversed that flow, with many Somalis now returning to their homeland. The arrivals are placing additional strain on local authorities in Puntland and Somaliland, who are already dealing with limited resources.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the developments are of particular concern. Many have family members who had been living in Yemen, and the new arrivals include relatives and friends. Diaspora communities have historically played a key role in supporting those affected by conflict, both in Somalia and abroad, and are now mobilizing to assist the newly displaced.\n\nThe situation remains fluid, and UNHCR has warned that the numbers could rise if the fighting continues. The agency has called for increased humanitarian access and funding to address the needs of those fleeing. Local authorities in Somalia have yet to issue a formal response to the arrivals, but humanitarian organizations are already on the ground providing basic assistance.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/23/dagaallada-yemen-oo-boqollaal-qof-kusoo-riixay-xeebaha-soomaaliya/",
