    "slug": "trump-addresses-un-on-iran-and-icc-erdogan-criticizes-lgbtq-policies",
    "summary": "US President Donald Trump defended military action in Iran and urged withdrawal from the ICC during his UN General Assembly speech, while Turkey's President Erdogan condemned international policies he says undermine the family.",
    "content": "On September 22, 2026, US President Donald Trump delivered a speech to the United Nations General Assembly in New York in which he defended American military operations in Iran and called on member states to withdraw from the International Criminal Court (ICC), according to Radio Dalsan. The address covered multiple foreign policy issues and marked a continued push by the Trump administration to challenge international institutions.\n\nTrump's remarks on Iran come amid ongoing tensions between Washington and Tehran. The US has conducted strikes against Iranian targets in recent years, and the ICC has opened investigations into possible war crimes related to those actions. Trump's call for countries to leave the ICC is part of a broader effort to shield American personnel from the court's jurisdiction. The ICC, which has over 120 member states, has faced criticism from several governments over perceived political bias.\n\nOn the same day, Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan spoke at the UN, focusing on what he described as international policies that weaken the family structure. Erdogan's comments appeared to target Western-backed LGBTQ+ initiatives, which his government has opposed. Turkey has recently intensified actions against LGBTQ+ groups, including restricting public events and media content. Erdogan's speech at the UN highlighted his willingness to challenge global norms on social issues.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments carry significant implications. Many Somalis have family members in the United States, and shifts in US foreign policy—especially regarding Iran and international legal bodies—could affect security and immigration dynamics. Additionally, Turkey is a key partner for Somalia, providing military training and humanitarian aid. Erdogan's stance on social issues may resonate with conservative segments of the diaspora, but it also raises concerns about human rights and international standing.\n\nThe speeches at the UN General Assembly reflect a growing divide between nations on issues of international justice and social values. As the global community navigates these tensions, the Somali diaspora will be watching closely, given its ties to both the United States and Turkey. The outcomes of these debates could shape policies that directly impact Somali communities abroad and in the Horn of Africa.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/09/23/trump-oo-un-ka-ka-hadlay-iran-iyo-icc/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=trump-oo-un-ka-ka-hadlay-iran-iyo-icc",
