    "original_title": "Eritrean exiles build network to topple Afwerki",
    "slug": "eritrean-exiles-in-tel-aviv-build-network-to-oppose-afwerki",
    "summary": "An Eritrean opposition group in Tel Aviv claims to have established the most organized diaspora network against President Isaias Afwerki, seeking support from the US, Israel, and Gulf states.",
    "content": "An Eritrean opposition movement operating from a warehouse in southern Tel Aviv has announced the creation of what it describes as the most organized diaspora network to date aimed at challenging the government of Eritrean President Isaias Afwerki. The group, known as Brigade N'Hamedu—which translates roughly to \"For the Homeland\" in Tigrinya—has reportedly established coordinated community structures across Israel, the United States, Canada, Sweden, Germany, and Ethiopia, according to a Haaretz report published on September 18, 2026.\n\nThe movement is actively seeking political backing from the United States, Israel, and Gulf states, the report states. Brigade N'Hamedu's efforts represent a significant escalation in diaspora-based opposition to Afwerki's long rule. Eritrea has been led by Afwerki since its independence in 1993, and his government has faced persistent criticism from international human rights organizations for its repression of dissent and indefinite national service, which has driven many Eritreans to flee the country.\n\nKey figures involved in Brigade N'Hamedu were not named in the source material, but the group's strategy of coordinating across multiple countries suggests a sophisticated approach to mobilizing the Eritrean diaspora. The network's presence in Ethiopia, which fought a devastating border war with Eritrea from 1998 to 2000, adds a regional dimension to its activities. The group's outreach to Gulf states may also reflect a broader geopolitical calculation, as several Gulf nations have shown interest in the Horn of Africa in recent years.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant due to shared experiences of conflict-induced displacement and the challenges of organizing politically across borders. Many Somalis have also built transnational networks to advocate for change in their homeland, and the Eritrean example may offer lessons in coalition-building and international engagement. Moreover, the Horn of Africa's stability is of direct concern to Somalia, given the region's interconnected security dynamics and the presence of Eritrean troops in Somalia in the past.\n\nThe Eritrean government has not responded to the report, and the effectiveness of Brigade N'Hamedu's efforts remains to be seen. However, its emergence highlights the persistent opposition to Afwerki's rule and the potential for diaspora groups to influence political outcomes in their home countries. As the movement seeks international support, its activities will likely be closely watched by both Eritrean authorities and regional powers.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/africa/eritrean-exiles-network-topple-afwerki/",
